// REJECTS: For input string: "abc"
//
// `port: 'abc';` in a MemSql datasource specification. The grammar types this field as
// STRING -- `port: 'abc'` is a perfectly well-formed string literal and the tree builds --
// and MemSqlGrammarParserExtension.visitMemSqlDatasourceSpecification then runs it through
// Integer.valueOf AT PARSE TIME (line 53). The message is java.lang.NumberFormatException's,
// leaking through with no source position at all.
//
// This is the single sharpest value-validation seam in the vendor grammars, for two reasons.
// It is invisible: nothing in the .g4 distinguishes this STRING from any other, and there is
// NO upstream grammar test for the MemSql datasource spec to copy the behaviour from. And it
// is inconsistent with the vendor next door: Databricks' `port` is also a STRING and is NOT
// converted -- `port: 'abc'` on a Databricks spec is ACCEPTED by this same runner (verified).
// Two adjacent grammars, identical field name, identical grammar type, different parse-time
// behaviour.
//
// `useSsl` in this same block is the same trap in Boolean form: a STRING parsed as a boolean.
// See ./neg-memsql-port-integer-literal.pure for the other half of the port story.
###Connection
RelationalDatabaseConnection fixture::neg::MemSqlBadPort
{
   store: fixture::neg::Db;
   type: MemSQL;
   specification: MemSql
   {
      host: 'localhost';
      port: 'abc';
   };
   auth: Test;
}
